{
  "schema": 2,
  "about": "The cut and sieve seed order plan/search.py chose per (L, n_batches) for the searched board: the shared cut points c_1 < ... < c_{k-1} that batching.partition takes, k = n_batches // 3, and the name of one of plan.recovery.seed_orders(widened=True). Replay with plan.search.searched_plan(L, n_batches, cut, seed_order); do not re-search. Each cell also records its frontier -- the feasible plans' Pareto set over (max_intermediates, flops), ascending in max_intermediates, with each point's plan and counts -- and 'selected', the index of the chosen plan in it, so a reader with another exchange rate between the two can re-select without re-searching (#171).",
  "objective": {
    "feasible": "max_targets <= minimax + minimax_tolerance",
    "minimax_tolerance": 1,
    "frontier": "the feasible plans whose (max_intermediates, flops) no other feasible plan's weakly dominates, one plan per distinct point",
    "select": "minimise max_intermediates / intermediates_min + flops / flops_min over the frontier (the minima are the frontier's), in exact rationals; equal scores to the fewer max_intermediates",
    "rule": "Among feasible plans (max targets within `MINIMAX_TOLERANCE` of the cell's minimax), take the Pareto frontier over (max_intermediates, FLOPs) and select the point minimising `intermediates / intermediates_min + flops / flops_min`, intermediates breaking ties. Equal weight because the paper prints the two in separate tables and trades them against each other without naming a price; normalised because the two quantities differ by an order of magnitude and an unnormalised sum would be a FLOPs-only rule wearing a disguise.",
    "ties": "one plan per point: the first in search order, lowest cut points, then seed_orders(widened=True) order"
  },
